{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"agent_message","text":"As of **6 November 2026**, Regulation 26(2)’s common dealing-deadline rule has express exceptions permitting different cut-offs for:\n\n- **Cash versus in-kind subscriptions/redemptions**—for example, an earlier cash cut-off and later in-kind cut-off.\n- **Classes with class-level currency hedging versus unhedged classes**—for example, an earlier hedged-class cut-off to accommodate FX execution. Portfolio-level hedging alone is not this exception.\n\nThese are **express regulatory permissions**, not merely applications of the older **UCITS Q&A 1030** approach. That Q&A provided a **case-by-case Central Bank waiver** from the common-deadline requirement; it was not blanket permission for arbitrary differences between classes or investors.\n\nI could not independently retrieve the 2026 legislative text here, so the examples above should not be read as establishing mandatory earlier/later sequencing.\n\n**Worth knowing:** dealing cut-offs govern primary-market fund orders, not ETF exchange-trading hours."}}
